-- Replace templates 3 and 4 with full user-provided instructions and section content.

UPDATE public.prd_templates
SET description = $desc$A comprehensive checklist to ensure accessibility compliance in design, content readability, and navigation for all users, including those with disabilities.$desc$,
    structure = $json${"instructions": "Reference specific WCAG 2.1 AA standards for each checklist item. Include practical testing methods (manual vs. automated) for each criterion. Organize by implementation priority so teams can tackle high-impact items first. Include code-level guidance where relevant (ARIA attributes, semantic HTML). Flag items that are commonly overlooked.", "sections": [{"name": "Accessibility Overview", "guidelines": "Scope and goals for this accessibility assessment.\n\n### Target Standard\n- WCAG Level: 2.1 AA (recommended minimum) / 2.1 AAA / Section 508\n- Legal requirements: ADA, EAA (European), AODA (Canadian), or other\n\n### Scope\n- Which pages, features, or flows are covered\n- Which user types: visual, auditory, motor, cognitive impairments\n- Which devices: desktop, mobile, tablet, screen reader\n\n### Tools\n- Automated: axe, Lighthouse, WAVE\n- Manual: Screen reader testing (VoiceOver, NVDA, JAWS)\n- User testing with people who use assistive technology"}, {"name": "Perceivable", "guidelines": "Can all users perceive the content? (WCAG Principle 1)\n\n### Images & Non-Text Content\n- [ ] All images have descriptive alt text (not 'image' or 'photo')\n- [ ] Decorative images use alt=\"\" or CSS background\n- [ ] Complex images (charts, diagrams) have extended descriptions\n- [ ] Icons with meaning have accessible labels\n\n### Video & Audio\n- [ ] Videos have captions (not auto-generated without review)\n- [ ] Audio content has transcripts\n- [ ] No content relies solely on audio or visual cues\n\n### Color & Contrast\n- [ ] Text contrast meets 4.5:1 ratio (normal text) and 3:1 (large text)\n- [ ] Information is never conveyed by color alone\n- [ ] UI components have 3:1 contrast against adjacent colors\n- [ ] Focus indicators are clearly visible\n\n### Responsive & Adaptable\n- [ ] Content is usable at 200% zoom\n- [ ] Content reflows at 320px width without horizontal scroll\n- [ ] Reading order is correct when CSS is disabled"}, {"name": "Operable", "guidelines": "Can all users operate the interface? (WCAG Principle 2)\n\n### Keyboard\n- [ ] All functionality is available via keyboard alone\n- [ ] Tab order follows a logical sequence\n- [ ] Focus is never trapped (except intentional modals with escape)\n- [ ] Skip-to-content link is present and functional\n- [ ] Custom components (dropdowns, modals, tabs) have proper keyboard support\n\n### Navigation\n- [ ] Page has descriptive <title>\n- [ ] Headings follow a logical hierarchy (h1 → h2 → h3, no skips)\n- [ ] Multiple ways to find content (nav, search, sitemap)\n- [ ] Breadcrumbs for multi-level navigation\n\n### Timing & Motion\n- [ ] No time limits, or users can extend/disable them\n- [ ] Auto-playing content can be paused or stopped\n- [ ] Animations respect prefers-reduced-motion\n- [ ] No content flashes more than 3 times per second\n\n### Touch & Pointer\n- [ ] Touch targets are at least 44x44px\n- [ ] Gestures have single-pointer alternatives\n- [ ] Drag-and-drop has keyboard alternatives"}, {"name": "Understandable", "guidelines": "Can all users understand the content and interface? (WCAG Principle 3)\n\n### Language & Readability\n- [ ] Page language is set with lang attribute\n- [ ] Content is written at an appropriate reading level\n- [ ] Abbreviations and jargon are explained on first use\n- [ ] Consistent terminology throughout the product\n\n### Predictability\n- [ ] Navigation is consistent across pages\n- [ ] Components behave consistently (same icon = same action)\n- [ ] No unexpected context changes on focus or input\n\n### Forms & Input\n- [ ] All form fields have visible labels (not just placeholders)\n- [ ] Required fields are clearly indicated\n- [ ] Error messages are specific: 'Email must include @' not 'Invalid input'\n- [ ] Errors are associated with the field using aria-describedby\n- [ ] Success and error states are announced to screen readers\n- [ ] Form instructions appear before the form, not just after\n\n### Error Prevention\n- [ ] Destructive actions require confirmation\n- [ ] Users can review before submitting important data\n- [ ] Undo is available where practical"}, {"name": "Robust", "guidelines": "Does the product work across assistive technologies? (WCAG Principle 4)\n\n### Semantic HTML\n- [ ] Proper use of landmarks: <nav>, <main>, <aside>, <footer>\n- [ ] Interactive elements use correct roles (`<button>`, <a>, not `<div onClick>`)\n- [ ] Lists use <ul>, <ol>, <dl> appropriately\n- [ ] Tables have <th>, scope, and <caption> where needed\n\n### ARIA Usage\n- [ ] ARIA is used only when native HTML is insufficient\n- [ ] aria-label and aria-labelledby are used correctly\n- [ ] aria-live regions announce dynamic content changes\n- [ ] Custom widgets follow ARIA authoring practices (combobox, dialog, tabs, etc.)\n\n### Screen Reader Testing\n- [ ] Tested with VoiceOver (macOS/iOS)\n- [ ] Tested with NVDA or JAWS (Windows)\n- [ ] Tested with TalkBack (Android)\n- [ ] All interactive elements are announced with correct name, role, and state"}, {"name": "Findings & Remediation Plan", "guidelines": "Issues found and the plan to fix them.\n\n### Issues\nFor each issue:\n- Issue: [Description]\n- WCAG criterion: [e.g., 1.4.3 Contrast]\n- Severity: Critical / Major / Minor\n- Location: [Page/component]\n- Current behavior: [What happens now]\n- Expected behavior: [What should happen]\n- Fix: [Specific code change or design update]\n\n### Priority Matrix\n| Severity | Count | Fix By |\n|----------|-------|--------|\n| Critical | [#] | Before launch |\n| Major | [#] | Within 30 days |\n| Minor | [#] | Within 90 days |\n\n### Process Improvements\n- Add accessibility to the design review checklist\n- Add automated accessibility tests to CI pipeline\n- Schedule regular manual audits (quarterly recommended)\n- Include accessibility in the definition of done\n\n### Resources\n- WCAG quick reference: https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/quickref/\n- ARIA authoring practices: https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/\n- Testing tools: axe DevTools, Lighthouse, WAVE"}]}$json$::jsonb
WHERE name = $name$Accessibility Compliance Checklist$name$ AND is_built_in = true;

UPDATE public.prd_templates
SET description = $desc$Plan your system architecture before you start building. Covers data model, API design, auth, integrations, and deployment — everything an AI coding agent needs to scaffold your app correctly from the start.$desc$,
    structure = $json${"instructions": "Write this as a technical blueprint that an AI coding assistant or engineer could use to scaffold the entire application. Be opinionated about technology choices — pick specific tools and explain why. Data models should list actual fields with types. API routes should specify methods, paths, and payloads. Auth should name the exact provider and flow. Keep it practical and buildable, not theoretical.", "sections": [{"name": "System Overview", "guidelines": "What the app does in 2-3 sentences, who it is for, and the high-level architecture.\n\n### Architecture Pattern\n- Type: Monolith / Microservices / Serverless / Jamstack\n- Key components: Frontend, Backend API, Database, Background Jobs, External Services\n\n### System Diagram\nDescribe how the major pieces connect: which services talk to which, what protocols they use (REST, GraphQL, WebSocket), and where data flows."}, {"name": "Data Model", "guidelines": "Define every core entity with its fields, types, and relationships.\n\n### Entity Format\n\nUser\n- id: uuid (PK)\n- email: text (unique)\n- name: text\n- role: enum (admin, member)\n- createdAt: timestamptz\n\n\n### Relationships\n- User → Project: One-to-many (user has many projects)\n- Project → Organization: Many-to-one (project belongs to org)\n- User ↔ Organization: Many-to-many (via Membership join table)\n\nNote which fields are indexed and any unique constraints beyond primary keys."}, {"name": "API Design", "guidelines": "List every API route grouped by resource.\n\n### Format\n\nMETHOD /api/path\nAuth: required | public\nBody: { field: type, ... }\nResponse: { field: type, ... }\n\n\n### Example\n\nPOST /api/projects\nAuth: required\nBody: { name: string, description?: string }\nResponse: { id, name, description, createdAt }\n\nGET /api/projects/:id\nAuth: required\nResponse: { id, name, description, members: [...] }\n\n\nNote rate limiting, pagination approach, and error response format."}, {"name": "Auth & Permissions", "guidelines": "### Authentication\n- Provider: e.g., Clerk, NextAuth, Supabase Auth\n- Methods: Magic link, OAuth (Google, GitHub), email/password\n- Session handling: JWT tokens, cookie sessions, etc.\n\n### Authorization\n- Roles: List each role and its capabilities\n- Enforcement: How auth is checked (middleware, per-route, RLS)\n- Public routes: Which pages/endpoints don't require auth\n\n### Multi-tenancy\nIf applicable: how organizations/workspaces are scoped and how data isolation works."}, {"name": "Third-Party Services", "guidelines": "List every external service the app depends on.\n\n### Format\nFor each service:\n- Service: What it does\n- SDK/Library: Which package to use\n- Config: Key environment variables and setup\n- Failure handling: What happens when this service is down\n\n### Common Services\n- Payments: Stripe, Lemon Squeezy, etc.\n- Email: Resend, SendGrid, Postmark\n- File storage: S3, Cloudflare R2, Uploadthing\n- AI/ML: OpenAI, Anthropic, Replicate\n- Analytics: PostHog, Mixpanel, Segment\n- Monitoring: Sentry, LogRocket"}, {"name": "Frontend Architecture", "guidelines": "### Tech Choices\n- Framework: Next.js, Vite + React, SvelteKit, etc.\n- Component library: shadcn/ui, Radix, Material UI, etc.\n- Styling: Tailwind CSS, CSS Modules, styled-components\n- State management: React Query, Zustand, Redux, etc.\n\n### Page Structure\nList every page/route with its path and purpose:\n- / — Landing or dashboard\n- /app — Main application\n- /settings — User/org settings\n\n### Data Fetching Strategy\n- Server components vs. client components\n- Caching and revalidation approach\n- Global loading and error state handling"}, {"name": "Infrastructure & Deployment", "guidelines": "### Hosting\n- Frontend: Vercel, Netlify, Cloudflare Pages, etc.\n- Backend: Same as frontend / Railway / Fly.io / AWS\n- Database: Neon, Supabase, PlanetScale, RDS\n\n### CI/CD Pipeline\n- Build and test steps\n- Preview deployments for PRs\n- Production deployment trigger (merge to main, manual, etc.)\n\n### Environments\n- Development: Local setup, seed data\n- Staging: Config and purpose\n- Production: Config, monitoring, alerts\n\n### Environment Variables\nList all required env vars by category (auth, database, services, feature flags). Note which are secret vs. public."}]}$json$::jsonb
WHERE name = $name$App Architecture Plan$name$ AND is_built_in = true;
